.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_PATH 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_path \- return the URL path of a request
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char *chttpsvr_req_path(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the NUL-terminated URL path of the request, without the query
string.  The string stays valid for the duration of the handler call.

The path always begins with "/": a request whose target is neither an
origin-form path nor an absolute-form http or https URL is answered
with 400 Bad Request before any middleware runs.  The one exception is
"OPTIONS *", which the server answers itself, with no middleware and
no handler; see chttpsvr_start(3).  For an absolute-form target the
path is the path of that URL, and an empty one becomes "/".

Percent-encoded sequences are decoded by the path rules of RFC 3986,
so the string is the path that was matched against the route
patterns.  A middleware that runs for a rejected request (see
chttpsvr_use(3)) can see a path whose percent-encoding is malformed,
which is itself one of the reasons why no route could match it.  Such
a path has no decoded form, so this function returns it exactly as it
arrived, in its encoded form.

The decoded path can contain a '/' that arrived as %2F, as well as "."
and ".." segments, which the server neither merges nor removes.  Never
use it as a file name without checking it first.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_raw_query (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_param (3)
